  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to coordinated project\napplications",
    "basePrintNo": "S5590",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5590-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 11,
       "fullName": "John Liu",
       "imgName": "1045_John_Liu.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1225,
       "sessionMemberId": 1223,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "LIU"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §273-a, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5590\n\nSPONSOR: MAYER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the education law, in relation to\ncoordinated project applications\n\n \nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nTo allow public libraries to submit coordinated project applications.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill amends section 273-a of the education law to allow libraries\nto submit single building project applications as well as a coordinated\napplication. This bill requires that applications submitted which\nrequest state aid in an amount greater than fifty percent must submit\ndocumentation demonstrating how the project will address the service\nneeds of one or more economically disadvantaged communities.  Addi-\ntionally, no more than one single building project application shall be\nsubmitted per year.\n\nThis bill allows public library systems to submit coordinated applica-\ntions. Each application shall be approved by the board of trustees of\neach participating library and library system. Each application must\ndemonstrate that resources are available to provide for maximum utiliza-\ntion of the project. Additionally, each application shall demonstrate\nthat the library operations would be more economical as a consequence of\napproval and only one coordinated project application shall be submitted\nper year.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Public Library Construction Grant program currently provides $34\nmillion in bonded funds to public libraries and library systems through-\nout the state for renovation and construction projects.  Currently\nlibraries can only submit one application targeting a single library.\nThis legislation would allow for library systems to submit applications\nfor single libraries as well as coordinated projects that would impact\nmultiple libraries, making it easier for these libraries to receive\nmuch-needed construction grants.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2018: A10835 (Barrett) - Passed Assembly\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone to the state. Local library systems who take advantage of this new\noption will see cost savings.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately, provided, however that the\namendments to section 273-a of the education law made by section one of\nthis act shall not affect the expiration and reversion of such section\nand shall be deemed to expire therewith.\n",
